actional distillation. It is used in high temperature welding, and in breathing. It commonly comes in the form of Oxygen, but is found as Ozone in the upper atmosphere. It was discovered by Priestley in 1774.'))],[-(s__subclass(s__Fluorine,s__ElementalSubstance))],[-(s__instance(s__Fluorine__t,s__SetOrClass))],[-(s__names(s__Fluorine,'Fluorine'))],[-(s__conventionalShortName('F',s__Fluorine))],[-(s__atomicNumber(s__Fluorine,"9"))],[s__instance(_1910336,s__Object),-(s__measure(_1910336,s__MeasureFn("18.9984",s__Amu))),s__instance(_1910336,s__Fluorine),s__instance(_1910336,s__Atom)],[-(s__documentation(s__Fluorine,s__EnglishLanguage,'A poisonous pale yellow gaseous element belonging to group 17 of the periodic table (The halogens). It is the most chemically reactive and electronegative element. It is highly dangerous, causing severe chemical burns on contact with flesh. Fluorine was identified by Scheele in 1771 and first isolated by Moissan in 1886.'))],[-(s__subclass(s__Neon,s__ElementalSubstance))],[-(s__instance(s__Neon__t,s__SetOrClass))],[-(s__names(s__Neon,'Neon'))],[-(s__conventionalShortName('Ne',s__Neon))],[-(s__atomicNumber(s__Neon,"10"))],[s__instance(_1910434,s__Object),-(s__measure(_1910434,s__MeasureFn("20.183",s__Amu))),s__instance(_1910434,s__Neon),s__instance(_1910434,s__Atom)],[-(s__documentation(s__Neon,s__EnglishLanguage,'Colourless gaseous element of group 18 on the periodic table (noble gases). Neon occurs in the atmosphere, and comprises 0.0018 percent of the volume of the atmosphere. It has a distinct reddish glow when used in discharge tubes and neon based lamps. It forms almost no chemical compounds. Neon was discovered in 1898 by Sir William Ramsey and M.W. Travers. '))],[-(s__subclass(s__Sodium,s__ElementalSubstance))],[-(s__instance(s__Sodium__t,s__SetOrClass))],[-(s__names(s__Sodium,'Sodium'))],[-(s__conventionalShortName('Na',s__Sodium))],[-(s__atomicNumber(s__Sodium,"11"))],[s__instance(_1910532,s__Object),-(s__measure(_1910532,s__Meas